part A: f(x) = 1/x^2, truncated integral from 1 to b, improper value 1
part A:       b       truncated value     error      b * error
part A:       10       0.9000000000  0.1000000000    1.0000
part A:      100       0.9900000000  0.0100000000    1.0000
part A:     1000       0.9990000000  0.0010000000    1.0000
part A:    10000       0.9999000000  0.0001000000    1.0000
part A: the error is exactly 1/b, so b times the error is 1 at every b in the table
part B: f(x) = 1/x^3, truncated integral from 1 to b, improper value 1/2
part B:       b       truncated value     error      b^2 * error
part B:       10       0.4950000000  0.0050000000    0.5000
part B:      100       0.4999500000  0.0000500000    0.5000
part B:     1000       0.4999995000  0.0000005000    0.5000
part B: the error is exactly 1/(2 b^2), so b^2 times the error is 1/2 at every b in the table
part C: f(x) = 1/x on [1, N], unit steps, lower sum H_N - 1 (floating point)
part C:      N = 2^m       H_N - 1      m / 2     H_N - 1 >= m/2
part C:          2    0.50000000     0.5000     True
part C:         16    2.38072899     2.0000     True
part C:        256    5.12434496     4.0000     True
part C:       4096    7.89510390     6.0000     True
part C:      16384    9.28130671     7.0000     True
part C: the bound H_N - 1 >= m/2 holds for m = 1 to 14; the bound m/2 grows without limit (Theorem 3.3)
